+# ASUS Crosshair X670E Extreme AniMe Matrix
+
+USB `0B05:1A21`, firmware family `ALDR2-S452-*`. Detection probes HID interface 1
+and rejects other firmware families sharing the controller PID. The separate
+Aura controller and LiveDash OLED are outside this driver's scope.
+
+## Exposed features
+
+* Off: `EC 42 00 00 00`, verified with `EC C1` status on the same HID handle.
+* Direct: 222 independently addressable RGB LEDs, software brightness 0..100.
+* Hardware Animation: resume the default device player; no slot/preset/upload UI.
+* One non-resizable matrix zone, portrait 7 columns × 36 rows with stepped edges.
+* Unchanged frames are suppressed. No polling timer, playback service or Qt
+  dependency is introduced by the controller.
+
+The row lengths are `2,4,6`, 26 full rows of 7, then `7,6,5,4,3,2,1`.
+Physical portrait placement is inferred from the owner's photograph and matching
+222-pixel topology. Exact asymmetric physical landmarks await owner confirmation.
+Wire identity and USB slot packing were retained from the locally inspected HAL
+table; the original oblique HAL image-space raster is not used as a physical map.
+
+## Protocol and ownership
+
+HID reports are 65 bytes. Firmware: `EC 82` -> `EC 02 00 <ASCII>`.
+Status: `EC C1` -> `EC 41`, byte 10 is player mode. Mode selection:
+`EC 42 mode 00 00` -> `EC 42 00` (0 off, 1 software, 2 hardware).
+Software frames: `EC 7F 04 00 03` -> `EC 7F 00`, then 768 bytes on bulk OUT `01`,
+interface 0. Mode 2 is not an off/S5 setting on the tested firmware.
+
+The frame has 16 blocks of 48 bytes (16 R, 16 G, 16 B). First block represents
+15 wire positions, subsequent blocks 14. Active wire IDs are 1..225 excluding
+213, 221, 225. `AsusMatrixRedOffset` maps one-based wire IDs to the R offset;
+G/B follow at +16/+32. All padding bytes remain zero.
+
+A private libusb context is initialized during early HID discovery, before
+legacy generic detectors can globally enable UsbDk on Windows. Shared ownership
+retains it across rescans and through device destruction. The bulk interface is
+opened lazily and its serial is matched before claim. No unrelated same-PID
+interface is claimed, no kernel driver is detached, and no firmware state is
+saved. HID preparation and bulk upload share a transaction mutex. Matching acks,
+lengths and bounded timeouts are checked. Off/readback uses the owned HID handle;
+do not open a competing diagnostic reader while this controller is active.
+
+## Validation and limitations
+
+The original full OpenRGB integration was confirmed by the owner for Off, uniform
+red and Direct. The isolated refactored transport was exercised on the Windows
+device: changed frame upload, repeated-frame suppression, Off/readback; no warnings.
+The proposed source files compile with MSVC and GCC/Linux against upstream
+headers. Simulated transport checks on both platforms cover serial-before-claim,
+duplicate frames, short bulk transfer recovery and invalid Off/readback/write.
+These are not claims of a full fresh OpenRGB build or physical Linux validation.
+
+Untested: first hotplug, S5/reboot persistence, exact asymmetric portrait landmarks,
+other firmware/model families, every preset/slot and persistent animation uploads.
+Image/GIF/APNG rendering belongs to an external companion/effects engine and is
+not included in this core driver change.
+
+Protocol references: [Polylux](https://github.com/vladulus/polylux), local HAL wire
+table inspection, and owner hardware observations. No vendor binary, disassembly,
+artwork, proprietary animation or third-party executable is included.
+
+AI assistance was used in this implementation. Manual human vetting under the
+upstream contribution policy remains outstanding; these checks do not replace it.
